    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "right, the TTL thing makes sense. but it's just frustrating because it feels like the data is actively being held back or something. is there any way to actually clear those caches or force them to refresh, or are we just totally at the mercy of whatever those resolvers decide to do?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "You're hitting the core frustration of DNS, which is that you have very limited direct control over every single resolver cache worldwide. You can't just send a \"clear cache\" command to every server on the planet. To try and speed things up, you can lower the TTL on your records *before* making the change, so the next update propagates faster, but for the existing stuck records, you are mostly at the mercy of their TTL expiry. It's a slow, decentralized process, not a single switch you can flip.",
